      "narrative": "Black Semiconductor, a Series A chip design company founded by Daniel and Sebastian Schall, is valued against a universe of 3 public semiconductor peers (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private-sector comparables. Within the private set, direct stage comparables include Rebellions ($2.3B Series A), d-Matrix ($2.0B Series A), and Positron AI ($1.0B Series B).\n\nTwo valuation methods are applicable. Stage-progression analysis uses the company's $254M total raised as a proxy base value and applies a 2.0x step-up multiplier typical for Series A to subsequent-stage transitions, yielding $509M. The peer-median approach anchors to the median valuation of three same-sector, same/adjacent-stage peers at $2.0B, with a range spanning $1.0B to $2.3B. A public-comparables approach (P/S multiple anchoring) is inapplicable; the public semiconductor median trades at 18.9x price-to-sales, but Black Semiconductor's revenue is not on file, precluding this triangulation.\n\nThe two applicable methods support a valuation range of $432M (low) to $2.3B (high), with a median of $1.3B. The wide spread reflects tension between early-stage capital deployed and peer valuations clustering toward $2.0B+. Key uncertainties include capital efficiency relative to the peer set and whether Black Semiconductor's technology differentiation warrants positioning toward the peer median or the more conservative stage-progression estimate.",
