      "narrative": "MacroFab, a Series C robotics and manufacturing company founded by Chris Church that has raised $141M to date, presents a valuation challenge across a fragmented peer set. The comparable universe spans 9 private and 1 public company in robotics and manufacturing, including stage-adjacent peers such as Exotec (Series D, $2.0B), Hadrian (Series D, $1.6B), Bedrock Robotics (Series B, $1.8B), and Dexterity (Series B, $1.6B).\n\nTwo triangulation methods apply. Stage-progression analysis, using total capital raised as a proxy base and applying a 1.7x multiplier typical for Series C advancement, yields $240M. Peer-median valuation across seven same-sector, same/adjacent-stage comparables produces $1.6B, with a range spanning $1.0B to $2.0B. The disparity reflects stage heterogeneity within the peer set\u2014several comps occupy Series B or Series D rather than Series C\u2014and varying operational maturity.\n\nThe triangulated analysis supports a valuation range of $204M (low) to $1.9B (high), with a median of $945M. This wide band reflects two material uncertainties: first, the stage-progression method's reliance on capital raised as a valuation proxy rather than revenue or profitability metrics; and second, the peer cohort's mixed stage distribution and limited public market comparables. Tightening this range would require revenue growth rates, customer concentration data, or unit economics specific to MacroFab.",
