      "narrative": "Nearfield Instruments is a Series D-stage semiconductor company founded by Roland van Vliet and Hamed Sadeghian, currently valued at $1.6B post-money with $527M+ cumulative capital raised. The valuation analysis draws on a peer universe of 8 private companies and 3 public semiconductors (ALAB, NVDA, AMD), all operating in the Chips & Semiconductors sector at Series C through Series E stages.\n\nTwo triangulation methods apply to this valuation. Stage-progression analysis, using a 1.5x multiplier typical of Series D advancement to next-stage funding, projects the $1.6B base to $2.4B. Peer-median anchoring across four same-sector, same/adjacent-stage comparables (Etched at $5.0B, Ayar Labs at $3.8B, Tenstorrent at $2.7B, and DEEPX at $529M) yields a median valuation of $3.2B, establishing a range of $529M to $5.0B. Public-comp anchoring via P/S multiples (18.9x median across the three public tickers) is not applicable, as Nearfield's revenue is not disclosed.\n\nThe triangulated analysis supports a valuation range of $2.0B (low) to $3.7B (high), with a median of $2.8B. This range reflects the tension between the company's last round valuation and peer comparables skewed higher by Series E-stage funding. Key uncertainties include Nearfield's revenue trajectory and competitive positioning within a peer set spanning a wide valuation spread, as well as stage-specific risk adjustments between Series D and later rounds.",
