      "narrative": "ACSL, a Tokyo-listed public company in Defense & Security founded by Kenzo Nonami, presents a valuation challenge due to limited direct comparables. The addressable peer universe comprises 26 same-sector entities\u20142 public (DRSHF, IDEAFORGE.NS) and 8 private firms\u2014but the narrow slice of same-stage and similar-tier peers falls below the three-datapoint threshold required for reliable peer-median triangulation.\n\nThe peer-median approach is inapplicable here. Filtering the 26 Defense & Security peers by stage and valuation tier yielded zero matches in same-stage, adjacent-stage, and similar-tier buckets respectively. An all-sector median would obscure material differences in defense-specific unit economics, customer concentration, and regulatory risk. The identified private peer set\u2014including Anduril Industries, Helsing, Castelion, Shield AI, and Saronic\u2014offers qualitative benchmarking context but lacks sufficient homogeneity for quantitative extrapolation.\n\nWithout sufficient narrow comparables and absent triangulated methods in the provided data, a specific valuation range cannot be supported here. The analysis underscores two key uncertainties: (1) whether ACSL's public-market pricing reflects a defense-sector discount or premium relative to private late-stage peers, and (2) whether Tokyo listing liquidity and regulatory environment command a valuation adjustment versus U.S.-listed or -unlisted defense firms. Additional data on revenue multiples, growth rates, or profitability across the peer set would be required to triangulate defensible valuation bounds.",
