      "narrative": "Revel, a Series B robotics and manufacturing company founded by Scott Morton, is evaluated against a comparable set of one public company and eight private peers within the frontier-tech robotics sector. The immediate peer cohort includes three same-stage Series B companies: Generalist AI ($2.0B), Bedrock Robotics ($1.8B), and Dexterity ($1.6B), with valuations spanning $1.6B to $2.0B.\n\nTwo valuation methods apply to Revel's $180M in total raised capital. The stage-progression method, applying a 1.9x multiplier typical of Series B advancement, yields $342M. The peer-median approach, drawing from the three robotics-focused Series B comparables, implies $1.8B valuation. These methods produce materially divergent signals, reflecting fundamental uncertainty about whether Revel's progress aligns with earlier-stage capital deployment or peers' later risk-adjusted valuations.\n\nThe triangulated range spans $291M (low) to $2.0B (high), with a median of $1.0B. This wide dispersion underscores competing narratives: the stage-progression method anchors to capital raised and assumes incremental step-up mechanics, while the peer-median approach reflects the elevated valuations established within the robotics cohort. Key uncertainties include the relevance of the peer set\u2014whether Generalist AI, Bedrock Robotics, and Dexterity adequately reflect Revel's technology differentiation and market traction\u2014and Revel's specific progress metrics relative to those benchmarks between Series B close and this valuation date.",
