      "narrative": "SF Compute is a Series A chip design company founded by Evan Conrad and Alex Gajewski. The valuation framework draws from a comp universe of 3 public semiconductor firms (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private-stage peers including Rebellions, d-Matrix, and Cerebras.\n\nTwo triangulation methods apply. Stage-progression anchors to SF Compute's $52M total raised, applying a 2.0x step-up multiplier typical for Series A-to-next-stage progression, yielding $104M. Peer-median analysis examines three same-sector, same/adjacent-stage comparables: Rebellions at $2.3B (Series A), d-Matrix at $2.0B (Series A), and Positron AI at $1.0B (Series B). The median of this cohort is $2.0B, with a range spanning $1.0B to $2.3B. Public-comp anchoring via P/S multiples (median 18.9x across the three public tickers) is not applicable owing to unavailable revenue disclosure from the target.\n\nThe triangulated range is $88M (low) to $2.3B (high), with a median of $1.1B. The wide spread reflects tension between the stage-progression method\u2014conservative given early-stage capital intensity\u2014and peer valuations, which command substantial premiums reflecting frontier-tech investor sentiment in AI-adjacent chip design. Key uncertainties include SF Compute's actual revenue trajectory, product differentiation versus named peers, and the duration of elevated valuations in private semiconductor markets.",
