      "narrative": "Azora is a Space & Aerospace frontier-tech company at an undisclosed stage with founders James Schalkwyk, Philip Mauskopf, and Martin Lavery. The valuation universe spans 13 identified peers\u20145 public-market comps (RKLB, PL, LUNR, ASTS, SATL) and 8 private entities including SpaceX, Rocket Lab, ICEYE, Sierra Space, and Planet Labs\u2014though meaningful narrow-stage clustering remains elusive.\n\nTwo standard valuation methodologies were tested. Peer-median triangulation proved inapplicable: while 14 same-sector peers exist in Space & Aerospace, filtering for identical stage, adjacent stage, or similar valuation tier yielded fewer than 3 datapoints in each category. Public-comp anchoring was similarly constrained: the sector median P/S ratio across the 6 public Space & Aerospace tickers is 24.4x, but Azora's revenue remains undisclosed, precluding revenue-multiple application.\n\nWithout disclosed revenue or a sufficiently dense comparable-stage cohort, conventional quantitative triangulation cannot be reliably executed. The analysis indicates the principal valuation constraint is information sparsity rather than methodology selection. Any forward valuation would require either (1) disclosure of Azora's revenue run rate or (2) identification of additional private-stage comparables within the same operational tier. The 24.4x sector P/S median serves as a general reference ceiling for public-market expectations, though its applicability to a private, earlier-stage operator remains uncertain pending revenue disclosure.",
